    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 052353\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n453 PM PDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Shower and thunderstorm chances increase across the Mojave Desert \n  on Sunday as moisture surges northward.\n\n* Drier conditions expected for Labor Day, though monsoonal\n  moisture and warmer temperatures return midweek.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Friday.\n\nThe primary forecast concern involves a moisture surge from \nHurricane Marie, currently located several hundred miles off the \nsouthern tip of the Baja Peninsula. Southwesterly flow aloft over \nthe southwestern United States will draw moisture from the system \ninto the region, bringing PWATs into the 1.0 to 1.5 inch range. The \napproach of a 500 mb perturbation in the flow pattern supports the \ndevelopment of a band of precipitation stretching southwest to \nnortheast along the Interstate 15 corridor. However, a slight shift \nto the north or south could bring much higher precipitation amounts \nto otherwise dry areas. Widespread cloud cover and the early morning \ntiming of the mid-level perturbation support widespread stratiform \nprecipitation rather than convective thunderstorms. Flow \nperpendicular to certain mountain ranges is an excellent setup for \norographic lift and the greatest precipitation totals are expected \nover high terrain. On the Spring Mountains, there is a 70 percent \nchance or greater of one inch of precipitation over 24 hours over \n8,000 feet in elevation and a 30 percent chance of two inches of \nprecipitation. Totals in the Las Vegas Valley will be far lighter, \nbut there is a 20 to 40 percent chance of at least one quarter inch \nof rain in 24 hours. Roadway ponding and running washes are possible \nacross much of the Mojave Desert. Precipitation should end on Sunday \nnight as the disturbance moves inland.\n\nWarmer and drier conditions are expected on Labor Day as heights \naloft start to increase again. High pressure will continue to build \nand move west through the workweek. The position and strength of the \nhigh will affect how much moisture is is able to draw into the \nwestern United States, from both tropical systems and traditional \nmonsoonal sources. Temperatures are also dependent on precipitation \nand cloud cover, but rising heights aloft should generally send \nhighs back towards normal values for early September.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...South-\nsouthwest winds with gusts between 20 and 25 kts expected into this \nevening before winds decrease to around 10 knots or less. Expect \ngradually increasing cloud cover this evening with clouds continuing \nto increase and lower overnight. Shower chances will develop as \nearly as 12Z, but more likely after 15Z with the potential of seeing \nlight to moderate rain from 15Z through 01Z. CIGs will generally \nremain around 10kft, but could drop to around 4kft at times during \nthe afternoon along with a 10% chance of visibility reductions less \nthan 6SM. Temperatures will remain below 100F through at least \nTuesday.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Gusty south-\nsouthwesterly winds expected into this evening this afternoon with \ngust speeds between 20 and 30 kts. The exception will be KDAG which \nwill gust from the west. Winds will gradually decrease this evening \nwith speeds generally less than 10 knots for most areas. Expect \ngradually increasing cloud cover this evening with CIGS likely \naround 10-15kft by morning for all the TAF sites except for KBIH \nwhere SCT-BKN high clouds are expected. Shower chances will start to \nincrease after 12Z for the Colorado River Valley TAF sites, KDAG, \nand Las Vegas Valley TAF sites, but as this rain band shifts north, \nit will mainly impact KDAG, KDRA, and the Las Vegas Valley TAF sites \nafter 15Z. CIGs generally around 10kft, but may lower between 4-6kft \nwith some heavier showers through the day. There is also a 10% \nchance of visibility reductions less than 6SM.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Meltzer\nAVIATION...Gorelow\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
